LDD(1)               UNIX Programmer's Manual               LDD(1)



NAME
     ldd - list a program's shared libraries

SYNOPSIS
     ldd file ...

DESCRIPTION
     Ldd runs each dynamic program file with LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS
     in its environment, and the binder in libc, rather than run
     the program, prints the libraries it needs, as SunOS's
     run-time linker does, and exits:

          -lc.1 => /lib/libc.so.1.0 (core $F6A3, prebound, resident)

     One line a library: its name and major, the file, the base its
     core is copied to in every process's data bank, whether the
     program bound to it prebound (the library is the one it was
     linked against) or by name, and whether it was resident or
     loaded now -- ldd makes an absent library resident, as running
     the program would.  A last line gives the bytes of other
     libraries' cores lying between the program's, which its data
     cannot use.  A library that cannot be found is listed as
     `not found'.

     A file that is not a dynamic program is reported as
     statically linked, or not an executable.

SEE ALSO
     ldload(8)

BUGS
     Ldd runs the program's startup, not just its loader: a file it
     cannot run is reported as an execution failure.

     A program whose library another process is loading waits
     for that load, for as long as the loading process is
     stopped; an interrupt ends the wait.

     Any user can make an installed library resident, until the
     bank pool needs its banks back.



